= MaterialX Volume Texture =

#context: vop
#type: node

"""Samples a volume texture atlas exported by Labs VolumeTexture Export."""

== Overview ==

Labs VolumeTexture Export writes a volume as a grid of slices packed into one
image. This node reads that image back, picking the two slices either side of
the shading point's height and blending between them.

The slice is chosen from the point's position inside the object's bounding box.
MaterialX has no access to a bounding box, so this node reads the box from two
primvars and works out the position itself. Author them on the geometry as
constant `float3` primvars holding the prim's `extent`:

{{{
primvars:bboxmin   float3   constant
primvars:bboxmax   float3   constant
}}}

Anything that reports a box will do; they do not have to come from `extent`.
Because they are constant primvars rather than shader parameters, one material
can be bound to many prims and each still uses its own box.

Export the atlas with __Up Axis__ set to Y, which is the axis this node slices
along.

@parameters

Volume Texture:
    #id: file
    The atlas image from Labs VolumeTexture Export.

U Tile:
    #id: utile
    Columns of slices in the atlas. Must match the export.

V Tile:
    #id: vtile
    Rows of slices in the atlas. Must match the export.

BBox Min Primvar:
    #id: bboxmin
    Name of the constant primvar holding the minimum corner of the box.

BBox Max Primvar:
    #id: bboxmax
    Name of the constant primvar holding the maximum corner of the box.

@outputs

out:
    The blended colour sampled from the two nearest slices.
